1
00:00:00,012 --> 00:00:04,820
But how close would an exploding star need
to be to completely strip away Earth's

2
00:00:04,820 --> 00:00:05,840
protective atmosphere?

3
00:00:06,580 --> 00:00:11,200
At peak optical brightness, a single
supernova can be comparable to an entire

4
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:12,000
galaxy.

5
00:00:12,631 --> 00:00:16,940
Within our Milky Way galaxy, these
violent stellar events occur roughly

6
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:21,400
1.6 to 4.6 times every century on average.

7
00:00:22,340 --> 00:00:27,480
Billions of years ago, ancient supernovae
formed heavy elements that enabled the

8
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:29,660
chemical processes for life on Earth.

9
00:00:31,377 --> 00:00:36,240
What would happen if a massive star exploded
only a few dozen light-years away from

10
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:37,040
our solar system?

11
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:42,700
Earth's atmosphere is remarkably effective
at absorbing high-energy electromagnetic

12
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:46,300
radiation like X-rays and gamma rays.

13
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:52,240
Surviving that initial burst of light might
make it seem like our planet escaped the

14
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:54,460
catastrophe completely unharmed.

15
00:00:55,043 --> 00:01:00,220
However, the real destruction is caused
by complex chemical reactions initiated

16
00:01:00,220 --> 00:01:02,080
in the upper layers of our air.

17
00:01:02,739 --> 00:01:07,900
High above us, intense energy from
incoming gamma rays begins breaking apart

18
00:01:07,900 --> 00:01:11,040
stable gas molecules in
the upper atmosphere.

19
00:01:12,748 --> 00:01:16,480
Incoming gamma radiation
causes radiolysis,

20
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:21,860
breaking apart standard diatomic nitrogen
and oxygen molecules high in the

21
00:01:21,860 --> 00:01:22,660
atmosphere.

22
00:01:23,041 --> 00:01:28,120
This reaction recombines molecular
nitrogen and oxygen into dangerous

23
00:01:28,120 --> 00:01:31,620
gaseous compounds known
as nitrogen oxides.

24
00:01:32,303 --> 00:01:36,620
Nitrogen oxides deplete protective
stratospheric ozone molecules.

25
00:01:37,455 --> 00:01:42,400
Computer models show that a nearby explosion
could cause a global ozone reduction

26
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:45,545
of 25 to 35 per cent.

27
00:01:45,995 --> 00:01:49,040
In specific regional locations
of the upper atmosphere,

28
00:01:49,660 --> 00:01:53,760
local ozone loss could reach
up to 75 per cent for years.

29
00:01:54,480 --> 00:01:59,660
Depleting this shield exposes Earth's
surface to bio-harmful solar ultraviolet

30
00:01:59,660 --> 00:02:03,620
radiation, causing widespread
biological damage.

31
00:02:05,213 --> 00:02:10,560
Supernovae expel several solar masses of
stellar material into space at speeds

32
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:13,020
reaching several per cent of light speed.

33
00:02:13,821 --> 00:02:15,560
In the case of a Betelgeuse supernova,

34
00:02:15,820 --> 00:02:20,760
the stream of charged particles
would reach Earth about 100,000 years

35
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:22,280
after the initial light.

36
00:02:23,961 --> 00:02:28,780
How close would a dying star actually
need to be to permanently destroy Earth's

37
00:02:28,780 --> 00:02:30,480
protective atmospheric shield?

38
00:02:31,260 --> 00:02:36,660
Scientists calculate dangerous stellar
distances using atmospheric computer models

39
00:02:36,920 --> 00:02:41,840
and measured radiation
flux from Supernova 1987A.

40
00:02:42,514 --> 00:02:47,380
Fortunately, a supernova explosion
occurring within 33 light-years of Earth

41
00:02:47,670 --> 00:02:52,020
is estimated to happen only
once every 240 million years.

42
00:02:52,747 --> 00:02:56,220
If a highly focused gamma-ray
burst pointed directly at Earth,

43
00:02:56,820 --> 00:03:01,079
its focused beam could vapourise
anything within 200 light-years.

44
00:03:01,509 --> 00:03:06,080
In fact, a nearby gamma-ray burst may
have triggered the Late Ordovician mass

45
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:07,220
extinction on Earth.

46
00:03:07,952 --> 00:03:13,420
That ancient cosmic event resulted in the
death of nearly 60 per cent of oceanic

47
00:03:13,420 --> 00:03:14,220
life on Earth.

48
00:03:15,905 --> 00:03:20,460
Are there any dangerous candidate stars
close enough to threaten Earth with an

49
00:03:20,460 --> 00:03:21,880
atmospheric collapse today?

50
00:03:22,586 --> 00:03:28,020
The famous red supergiant star Betelgeuse
sits at a distance of roughly 400

51
00:03:28,020 --> 00:03:30,660
to 600 light-years from our Sun.

52
00:03:31,336 --> 00:03:36,320
When Betelgeuse detonates as a brilliant
supernova within the next 100,000 years,

53
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:39,248
it will shine as bright as a half-Moon.

54
00:03:39,717 --> 00:03:42,140
Because Betelgeuse is
hundreds of light-years away,

55
00:03:42,740 --> 00:03:46,780
life on Earth will be completely
unharmed by its eventual explosion.

56
00:03:47,765 --> 00:03:52,340
The closest known candidate for a
white dwarf supernova is IK Pegasi,

57
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:55,000
but it is moving away to a safe distance.

58
00:03:55,795 --> 00:04:00,680
Even if other famous stars a few hundred
light-years away explode in 1,000 years,

59
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:02,500
Earth is in little danger.

60
00:04:03,327 --> 00:04:04,640
As you gaze up at the night sky,

61
00:04:05,140 --> 00:04:09,120
how does it feel knowing our atmosphere
keeps us safe from distant cosmic

62
00:04:09,120 --> 00:04:09,920
explosions?
